  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 238 PM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nThe high heat and humidity continues through at least Thursday. \nConfidence in the hot conditions Wednesday through Thursday remain \nhigh, given the latest ECMWF EFI/Shift of Tails values for high \ntemperatures are in excess of 0.8 to 0.9 (even some values over 0.95 \nin isolated spots), along with Shift of Tails values around 1. This \nsuggests high confidence in anomalously warm high temperatures, \nalthough not extreme values based on the lower Shift of Tails. With \nthe potential for these anomalously hot conditions to last this \nweek, we've decided to extend the Heat Advisory another day (through \nThursday). It's entirely possible for the advisory to be extended \ninto Friday, given LREF ensemble exceedance probabilities of 100 \ndegree heat indices exceeding 50% across our southern two tiers of \ncounties. Additionally, overnight lows might not dip below 75 \ndegrees through Wednesday night, so this would compound the \nheat stress - even at night. There is potential for some record \nwarm low temperatures during this period. See the Climate \nsection below for the current records.\n\nIn terms of thunderstorm activity for mid- to late-week, with the \nstrongest part of the heat building for Wednesday, seems like the \nstorm track will stay to our north. However, there is a signal for \nthe edge of the heat dome to sink slightly southward Thursday and \nFriday, generating more active conditions during this time frame. \nNBM consensus model blends seem to keep the chances of precipitation \nnorth of the I-80 corridor (20-50% chances) for Thursday and Friday, \nso the extreme heat could still be in play, even into Friday. \nUncertainty remains on the impact this activity will have on the \nheat potential for the northern forecast area, so stay tuned for \nupdates throughout the week!\n\nA look ahead to the upcoming weekend: the heat appears to diminish \nby the weekend, although there are some signals it will be more \nactive, with additional chances of showers and storms. In terms of \nsevere weather, this potential appears to be pretty low, given the \nlower probabilities from the various machine-learning severe prob \nresources.",
